00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.800
ELISSE ROCHE: So
another way to express

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.990
the idea of data science
for social change

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:43.032
is data for good, which is
interdisciplinary movement

00:00:43.032 --> 00:00:44.490
where private
companies are working

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:48.990
with nonprofit organizations,
NGOs, and also IGOs

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.330
in order to apply
big data solutions

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.690
to real-world challenges.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.260
And this has been going
on for quite some time.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.780
A couple of highlights
include, back in 2008,

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.320
the CDC collaborated with Google
to launch Google Flu Trends

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.470
under the idea that if you could
actually track online searches,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:09.120
you would potentially be able to
predict the prevalence of a flu

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.050
outbreak.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:13.200
Later, in 2011, the United
Nations Global Pulse Initiative

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.780
advocated for a data
philanthropy movement

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.960
where private companies could
actually donate or share data

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.330
to advance humanitarian causes.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.600
And in 2013, the UN also
utilized satellite imagery

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.580
in order to provide
targeted disaster relief

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.070
by comparing pre-
and post-disaster

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.710
imagery to determine which
communities needed emergency

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.150
relief first.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.992
And in 2017, just
last year, the UN

00:01:35.992 --> 00:01:37.690
hosted the world's
first World Data

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.390
Forum, with the
belief that it would

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.030
help to foster collaboration
between different organizations

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.280
and sectors and
also help to achieve

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.000
the 17 sustainable development
goals which comprise the 2030

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:48.990
Agenda

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:53.400
And in 2018, today, there are
interdisciplinary programs

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.580
and collaboration.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.520
So companies like
Google Cloud are

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.010
working with organizations
like DataKind

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.355
in order to bring the data
for good movement to life.

00:02:01.355 --> 00:02:02.730
JAKE PORWAY: So
I think what's so

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.620
fascinating is that
this is still very

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.360
young in a lot of ways, too.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.479
There hasn't yet been a
killer app for social impact,

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:10.740
and there probably won't be.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.590
Because though groups have
started to find solutions

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.300
like these, really
what I think people

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.550
are learning in these
first five or six years

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.770
that this has been up is
that collaboration is really

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:20.371
the key.

00:02:20.371 --> 00:02:22.620
Technologists don't always
know exactly what to build.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.060
Social organizations don't
have the tech skills.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.700
And so I think this
last point here

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.240
is so critical-- that for all
the cool, whiz-bang solutions

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.030
we're seeing, collaboration
and interdisciplinary work

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.100
is really key to this
movement growing.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.170
ELISSE ROCHE: Absolutely.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.960
So what is Google Cloud doing
in the data for good movement?

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.190
Well, since 2008, we have
sought to empower nonprofits

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.190
by offering essentially
integrated solutions for them

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:50.370
to access, starting with
Google Earth Outreach,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.140
allowing them to
essentially bring

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.150
their stories to
life using premium

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.730
Maps APIs and Geo Tools.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.950
With G Suite for
Nonprofits also, nonprofits

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.440
are able to work and collaborate
together with productivity

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.890
tools using Gmail
and Docs at no charge

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.590
and also GCP public data
sets, which actually

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:11.150
helps to democratize access
to planetary-scale data

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:15.090
sets provided by organizations
like the World Bank, the EPA,

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.810
and NOA, just to name a few.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.255
And now anyone can
essentially query that data up

00:03:20.255 --> 00:03:23.070
to one terabyte
per month for free.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.740
And last but not least, there
is Kaggle and their data science

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.600
for good events, which
allows nonprofits

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.410
to host an online event and to
tap into the community of over

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.200
1.5 million data
scientists in order

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.060
to apply big data solutions
to real-world problems.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.430
And in addition to
that, we're exploring

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:42.930
working with nonprofit
organizations

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.780
in a hands-on capacity,
like with DataKind.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.880
So back in April, we
actually worked with them

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.490
around their ecosystem event
at the Skoll World Forum

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.110
for Social
Entrepreneurship where

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.150
we discussed the potential
of applying big data

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.070
solutions to
real-world challenges

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.950
in the humanitarian sector and
also worked with nonprofits

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.770
to help scope potential
data analytics projects

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.270
for future engagements.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.280
And, in addition to that,
just a couple of weeks

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.380
ago, we hosted one of
their DataDives, which

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.662
is a weekend-long
problem-solving event

00:04:09.662 --> 00:04:12.120
at the Google New York City
office, which is actually where

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.797
we're streaming from today.

00:04:13.797 --> 00:04:15.630
And we'll get into that
in a bit more detail

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:16.881
later on in the presentation.

00:04:16.881 --> 00:04:19.380
But before we dive in there, I
wanted to rewind and actually

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.595
talk about DataKind, who they
are, and what they stand for.

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:23.470
JAKE PORWAY: Awesome.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:24.095
Thanks, Elisse.

00:04:24.095 --> 00:04:25.860
So a little background
on Data Kind

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.110
is that we're a nonprofit
dedicated to harnessing

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:31.650
the power of data science and
AI in the service of humanity.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.780
And the organization
really arose

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.020
from experiencing lots of data
scientists, machine learning

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.990
folks, AI engineers who
clearly wanted to give back

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:42.790
but didn't exactly know
how, and at the same time,

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:45.100
realizing that social
organizations are awash

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:45.600
in data.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:46.916
I mean, we all are, right?

00:04:46.916 --> 00:04:48.540
There's digital
information popping off

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.820
of our cell phones
and our laptops.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.560
And so we have these
new opportunities

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.227
to learn more about our world
and ourselves than ever before.

00:04:56.227 --> 00:04:57.810
And so really, we
thought the best way

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:01.890
to do that was not just to have
industry building what it's

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:03.570
building with data
science and machine

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:06.630
learning but finding ways to get
those same tools into the hands

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.310
of people working
in social impact,

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.310
whether those are governments
or nonprofits or anyone

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.770
else dedicated to
seeing a better world.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.960
And as a data scientist
myself, it started just

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.820
by talking to a few
folks and saying, hey,

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.584
does anyone else want to
work with some nonprofits,

00:05:20.584 --> 00:05:22.500
and being very surprised
to find that not only

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.240
did people in my own
friend group want to do it.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.790
But we had thousands of people
around the world writing

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.470
and saying, hey, I
want to do this, too,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.386
nonprofits and governments
raising their hands

00:05:30.386 --> 00:05:33.496
and saying, we want to be part
of the data for good movement.

00:05:33.496 --> 00:05:35.120
So that's how the
organization started.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.520
And we run it a lot like Doctors
Without Borders for data geeks.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.520
We just get folks who
want to volunteer and give

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:42.350
their time back to work
and build solutions

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.150
alongside nonprofits.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:46.550
In the last six years, we've
done about 250 projects.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.530
We've got about
18,000 folks signed up

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:51.170
as volunteers and a bunch
of communities running

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:54.050
DataKind versions in their own
cities as volunteer chapters.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:57.410
So I'm very excited to see this
data for good movement growing

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:59.319
immensely over the
last couple of years.

00:05:59.319 --> 00:06:01.610
I'm going to give you just
a few quick examples of what

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.780
some of the projects look like.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:06.800
So one was a project we did
with the American Red Cross.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.695
They're a fantastic
organization.

00:06:08.695 --> 00:06:10.820
And one of the challenges
they were trying to solve

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:14.000
was how to stop
preventable fires.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.875
So there's a problem
where if a fire goes off

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:17.916
and people don't have a
smoke alarm in their home

00:06:17.916 --> 00:06:19.640
or in their buildings,
they can get hurt.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:22.010
Or worse, they can die
because they can't get out.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.510
So the Red Cross
decided we're going

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.607
to put millions of smoke alarms
for free around the country.

00:06:26.607 --> 00:06:28.190
The question was,
where do they start?

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:29.317
Where should you put these?

00:06:29.317 --> 00:06:31.400
So we teamed them up with
some data scientists who

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:34.550
combined their data about
fire data along with publicly

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:37.030
available data from
community housing data

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.870
or open data that
others had made

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:40.970
available to build a
predictive model that

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.860
said these are the places
where if you put smoke alarms

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.310
they're going to be most
effective for saving lives.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:48.014
You can see that
little map on the slide

00:06:48.014 --> 00:06:49.430
shows the areas
where they thought

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.638
there would be the most
fires and the most folks that

00:06:51.638 --> 00:06:52.880
could be benefited from this.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:55.100
So now, the Red Cross
has intelligence to say,

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:58.690
here's where we can go to
focus our limited resources.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:00.920
And they're now saving
hundreds of lives per month

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:02.085
with this algorithm.

00:07:02.085 --> 00:07:03.980
So that's one great example.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:05.780
Another example
about a group trying

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:08.280
to figure out where to use
their limited resources

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.280
is with the Moulton
Niguel Water District.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.434
Now, they give water to folks
all across Southern California.

00:07:13.434 --> 00:07:15.350
And their job depends
on being able to predict

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:16.910
how much demand there is.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:19.140
And the stakes are high
because if they're wrong,

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:20.930
the only way they
have to solve this

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:23.960
is to go take a dump truck,
fill it up full of water

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.090
across town, and ship
it to the people that

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.200
need the water
wherever it's missing.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:29.360
And that's a huge cost.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:30.734
It's environmentally
destructive.

00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:32.090
It's a bad thing.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:34.010
So some data scientists
teamed up with them

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:35.504
and saw if they could do better.

00:07:35.504 --> 00:07:36.920
And basically,
they built the tool

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.128
that you're seeing in that
picture on the slide which

00:07:39.128 --> 00:07:42.350
is able to predict demand
to the block-by-block level.

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:44.840
And now they're not only able
to get more water to millions

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:49.070
of people in California,
they've saved over $25 million

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:52.799
just saved from shipping costs
and from being more efficient.

00:07:52.799 --> 00:07:54.590
So I think these are
great examples of what

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:57.080
can happen when you use
data science and machine

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:00.500
learning and prediction to help
social organizations target

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:03.020
their efforts more
cleanly and better.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.260
So that is largely the
work that was done there.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.260
But now you may ask, well,
how does this happen?

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:08.600
We've got a couple
of different models

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:10.933
for how we get data scientists
together with nonprofits.

00:08:10.933 --> 00:08:12.950
But one is our DataDive.

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:16.160
And DataDives are like
social impact hackathons.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.560
We love that hackathons are
high-energy to get a lot done.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.980
But we try to modify them
so that we really made sure

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.500
that by the end of these
events, social organizations

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.780
and nonprofits had something
that they could use.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:30.089
So for our DataDives, basically,
we've got three phases.

00:08:30.089 --> 00:08:31.130
The first is exploration.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:33.830
So we worked for a while with
these nonprofits figuring out

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:35.990
what their problems
are, where the data is,

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:37.465
what can and can't be done.

00:08:37.465 --> 00:08:38.840
A lot of organizations
don't even

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:40.262
know what's possible at first.

00:08:40.262 --> 00:08:41.720
So we build that
out so that we can

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.730
have really clear
scopes and clear data

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.270
so that, in the second
phase during this big event,

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:50.030
hundreds of volunteers could
show up and just start working.

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:51.946
They could hack away at
a problem they wanted.

00:08:51.946 --> 00:08:54.630
We had the nonprofits there that
they could sit and talk with.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.750
And so for 48 hours, they
worked on these problems

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:59.540
so that in stage three, we
can hand off those solutions

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.090
to partners and find
that groups didn't just

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:05.360
come up with interesting
ideas or a few creative starts

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:07.730
like you often see in
hackathons but actually made

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:10.760
real progress on their goals so
that the nonprofits could all

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:13.820
walk away with something
that they could use.

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:16.130
So that was basically the
setup for how we work and we

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:17.410
did with the DataDive.

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:20.369
And now, we'll talk a little
bit about the event itself.

00:09:20.369 --> 00:09:21.410
ELISSE ROCHE: Absolutely.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:24.110
So from the Google
Cloud side, we actually

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:25.970
realized the potential
of this kind of model

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:27.470
to drive real-world
impact, which

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:29.300
is why we hosted
one of the DataDives

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.100
in our Google New
York City office,

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:34.360
drawing over 130 attendees
from data scientists

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:37.430
to analysts to designers all
passionate about defending

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:41.072
human rights with data and also
building resilient communities.

00:09:41.072 --> 00:09:42.530
The nonprofit
organizations that we

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:44.330
worked with over the
course of that weekend

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.670
all worked around international
development projects

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.800
or in defending
different communities

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:51.380
from the harmful effects of
international development

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:52.160
projects.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:54.770
And these three organizations
were Inclusive Development

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:58.340
International, Accountability
Council, and also International

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:00.152
Accountability Project.

00:10:00.152 --> 00:10:01.610
And over the course
of the weekend,

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.190
they were actually able to
make significant progress

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:06.300
on the challenges
that they posed

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.210
and walk away with
tangible solutions

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:10.490
that they would be able to
then integrate immediately

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:13.370
into their processes,
which is truly inspiring.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:14.930
Because you actually
see that you

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:16.610
can sit down in a
room full of computers

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:19.844
with a roomful of very talented
and technical data scientists

00:10:19.844 --> 00:10:21.260
and then take that
and immediately

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:24.209
throw it into the real
world and have that impact.

00:10:24.209 --> 00:10:26.000
And in addition to all
of that, we actually

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.400
had the opportunity to host
a Google Cloud breakout

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:31.460
session where we discussed
the currently available tools

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.800
and resources for
nonprofit organizations

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.540
and how they can access them
through Google for Nonprofits,

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:40.420
Kaggle, and also GCP
public data sets.

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:42.670
So on the projects themselves
and the nonprofits--

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:43.420
JAKE PORWAY: Yeah.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:44.350
So let's talk about these folks.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:45.871
As Elisse mentioned,
they're all in

00:10:45.871 --> 00:10:47.620
the international
development space, which

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:49.536
means that all these
organizations are focused

00:10:49.536 --> 00:10:52.360
on defending communities
who need help representing

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.820
their rights or
getting representation

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:57.700
when international development
is happening in their area.

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:00.115
But they each tackle a very
different piece of this.

00:11:00.115 --> 00:11:01.990
I thought that was kind
of interesting as you

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:03.699
listen to the three projects.

00:11:03.699 --> 00:11:05.740
So the first was the
International Accountability

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:06.550
Project.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:09.850
And their whole purpose is to
advocate, make policy change,

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:12.580
boost local advocacy efforts
to make sure that communities

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:15.130
know about every international
development project happening

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:17.890
around them and
could act on them

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:19.880
or defend themselves
when needed.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:21.814
Now, they're a pretty
visionary organization.

00:11:21.814 --> 00:11:23.230
I love these folks
because they're

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:26.560
already data-savvy in the sense
that they have worked really

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:29.860
hard to collect a whole
database of every international

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:32.170
development project they
know of that's going on.

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:34.060
So they could tell
you right now what's

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:37.391
going on in Peru or in Ghana
and who's responsible for it.

00:11:37.391 --> 00:11:39.640
So that's really great, and
they've already done that.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.515
The challenge that they
had for this weekend,

00:11:41.515 --> 00:11:43.510
however, is that
they were trying

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:46.120
to understand how they could
get more updated information

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:47.450
about what's going on.

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:49.450
So there are news
articles or press releases

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:51.790
that happen around
these projects,

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.020
but they just go
off into the ether.

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:57.649
So IAP thought to themselves,
if we have this information,

00:11:57.649 --> 00:11:59.440
maybe we could have an
early warning system

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:01.356
when we know there's
going to be a new project

00:12:01.356 --> 00:12:02.710
or when it's going to change.

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:05.350
But with that would require is
scraping huge amounts of news

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:06.980
information and matching it.

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:09.070
So that's what the
volunteers set about doing.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.330
They basically scraped tons
of news articles and press

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:11.830
releases.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:13.704
They built some semantic
matching algorithms,

00:12:13.704 --> 00:12:15.790
some classifiers to
try to understand

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:19.277
which projects these articles
reacted to or related to.

00:12:19.277 --> 00:12:20.860
And then they pulled
that all together

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:23.290
into one workflow that
actually now pulls that data,

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.450
tags it, and puts
it in this database.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:28.180
And so now IAP is walking
away with a whole API

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:30.910
on top of this new data
model that basically gets

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:34.510
current events and news
media into their system

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:36.440
so they can act
more effectively.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.470
Now this allows them to
get ahead of changes,

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:40.760
get communities ready before
they ever could before.

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:43.010
So it's kind of like having
early sight, which I think

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:43.976
is awesome.

00:12:43.976 --> 00:12:45.119
So that was IAP.

00:12:45.119 --> 00:12:45.910
That was one group.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:48.530
Fantastic work, and they're
going to be continuing that on.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.113
The second group we
want to talk about

00:12:50.113 --> 00:12:52.070
is Inclusive Development
International.

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:55.180
Now, Inclusive Development
International, or IDI,

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:58.160
they're basically focused
on following the money.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:00.160
So whenever there's an
international development

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:02.429
project-- say you're
building a mine in Ghana--

00:13:02.429 --> 00:13:03.970
they want to know
all of the funders.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:07.030
Who are the banks and
investors in these projects?

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:08.500
So then if something
does go wrong,

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:10.467
if there's an
unethical activity,

00:13:10.467 --> 00:13:12.800
they can understand who those
funders are and go to them

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:14.383
and say, hey, did
you know about this?

00:13:14.383 --> 00:13:15.870
Maybe we should
take some action.

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:17.120
Now, that's all well and good.

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:19.990
But if you think about what it's
like to be IDI, when they want

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:21.522
to know who all
these funders are,

00:13:21.522 --> 00:13:23.980
they have to basically go out
and find all that information

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:24.850
each time.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:27.170
So you have to go to each
development bank's website.

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:29.330
You got to type in the
development project's name.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:32.080
You got to get that information,
copy it, paste it, et cetera.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:35.200
It's this huge,
laborious human project.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:37.300
So what they were
focusing on this weekend

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:40.150
was trying to figure out if they
could automate that process.

00:13:40.150 --> 00:13:43.270
So could they basically
automatically scrape this data

00:13:43.270 --> 00:13:45.590
and put it all into one
workflow so that they could

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:48.364
have this at a moment's notice?

00:13:48.364 --> 00:13:49.780
So that's what
they set out to do.

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:53.830
And miraculously, in the just
48 hours of this weekend,

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:56.310
they set out to
build 13 scrapers.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:59.620
The team built 11 scrapers in
that time that were all working

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:01.210
collecting that information.

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:02.877
So they can now scrape
that information.

00:14:02.877 --> 00:14:04.585
There's a front end
that they're building

00:14:04.585 --> 00:14:06.460
that now allows IDI
to go in and just type

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:09.510
that project name, get all
that information in one click,

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:10.960
basically go about
doing the work

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:12.150
that they really need to do.

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:14.831
So it's saving their
time from this human join

00:14:14.831 --> 00:14:17.080
that they're basically doing
and instead automating it

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:18.992
so that they can focus
on the work ahead.

00:14:18.992 --> 00:14:20.700
So they're going to
be building that out.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:22.330
And they were really
excited to say

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:24.163
that they could just
immediately start using

00:14:24.163 --> 00:14:25.810
this right after the weekend.

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:27.210
ELISSE ROCHE: That's great.

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:29.260
And Accountability
Council-- so they actually

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:31.360
work around amplifying
the voices of communities

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:33.070
around the world to
help protect them

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:35.380
from the harmful effects of
international development

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:36.340
projects.

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:38.285
And an example of this
that's worth noting

00:14:38.285 --> 00:14:39.910
is that they actually
have been working

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:41.740
with a series of
villages in Ukraine

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:44.800
who are facing the harmful
effects of industrial farming

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:47.712
where they're living with
17 million chickens housed

00:14:47.712 --> 00:14:50.170
in a series of structures that
extend as far as the eye can

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:50.670
see.

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:53.060
And there's actually a picture
of this on the next slide.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:54.830
And so the
Accountability Council

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:56.740
is actually working
with them to fight back

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:59.260
against the company and
the development banks that

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:01.150
are funding them
in order to address

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:04.270
the pollution, the water
scarcity, and the pesticide use

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:07.660
that all result from
intensive animal farming.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:11.020
And the challenge that they
posed to the DataKind community

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:13.960
during the DataDive was
analyze online complaints

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:16.240
and identify which aspects
of these complaints

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:18.040
actually determine success.

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:20.920
Is it something as simple as
the time in which they're filed?

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:26.067
Is it the number of valid issues
associated with the complaint?

00:15:26.067 --> 00:15:27.400
So they dug a little bit deeper.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:33.010
And they analyzed a series
of projects and data

00:15:33.010 --> 00:15:34.630
across a variety of
different websites

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:37.100
and then consolidated that
in one website in order

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:37.859
to analyze that.

00:15:37.859 --> 00:15:39.400
And from there, they
actually came up

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:42.960
with a series of high-impact
insights, one of which

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:45.630
is actually pictured on
the slide, where they noted

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:49.290
that there is more
than a 50% chance

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:51.870
that a complaint would become
eligible to be addressed

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:53.730
if it has three issues or more.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:55.605
So that's something that
they can immediately

00:15:55.605 --> 00:15:57.600
start integrating
into their processes

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.011
and consider when
filing these complaints.

00:16:00.011 --> 00:16:02.010
If they just have two but
there's an opportunity

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:03.480
to add a third one,
that would then

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:07.600
help to ensure the success
of that particular complaint.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:10.360
And so it's worth noting
that at the end of the day,

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:12.522
we're talking about real
people with real problems.

00:16:12.522 --> 00:16:13.980
And this data and
this kind of work

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:16.230
that we're doing over
a DataDive weekend

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:17.890
can have immediate impact.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:18.640
JAKE PORWAY: Yeah.

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:20.872
And I think that just adding
to that, sometimes when

00:16:20.872 --> 00:16:22.830
people hear these examples,
they're like, yeah,

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:25.470
but that's not scale.

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:29.520
That's not millions of people
affected or terabytes of data

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:30.300
analyzed.

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:31.182
Sometimes it's not.

00:16:31.182 --> 00:16:32.640
But really, like
Elisse was saying,

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:36.460
these are people's lives
that are affected by this.

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:40.230
And so if Accountability Council
can get three more complaints

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:43.020
through, when you're that
community or that family that

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:46.090
is looking for help, getting
that through versus not

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:47.580
is a huge issue.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:48.990
That could be life or death.

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.690
So I think that's what
I hear in all of these--

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:55.380
is just that people's lives
are being saved and helped

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:57.020
so tangibly from this work.

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:58.061
ELISSE ROCHE: Absolutely.

00:16:58.061 --> 00:17:00.270
And I think one of the
villages in Ukraine filing

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.020
that complaint with
Accountability Council

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:04.710
described them as one
of their last hopes.

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:05.460
JAKE PORWAY: Yeah.

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:07.829
And so I think that's why
we're so excited by what

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:09.450
we're seeing in these events--

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:11.609
is that all these
organizations are now

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:13.349
boosting their missions
with data science

00:17:13.349 --> 00:17:14.440
and machine learning.

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:17.069
So one of the takeaways we see
is just the shocking amount

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:19.800
that gets done in 48 hours.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:22.619
You may think that if you're a
nonprofit that a data science

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:24.491
project has to take
years upon years.

00:17:24.491 --> 00:17:25.740
You got to build a team first.

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:28.470
But we see organizations that
over a few months and then

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:30.810
a quick weekend
make huge progress

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:32.182
just by getting folks together.

00:17:32.182 --> 00:17:34.140
And similarly, data
scientists may think, well,

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:34.806
what could I do?

00:17:34.806 --> 00:17:35.640
Well, there's a lot.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:37.889
You can make a big difference
in a little bit of time.

00:17:37.889 --> 00:17:39.630
And so that's, I think,
really exciting--

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:42.171
is that when the right people
come together in the right way,

00:17:42.171 --> 00:17:43.152
you get a lot done.

00:17:43.152 --> 00:17:45.110
The other thing, though,
is that of course it's

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:46.230
not just a weekend.

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:47.970
We're not going to solve
every problem in a weekend.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:48.720
It would be foolish.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:49.575
ELISSE ROCHE: As much
as we would like to.

00:17:49.575 --> 00:17:51.690
JAKE PORWAY: Yeah, as much
as we'd all love that,

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:53.680
these are systemic,
endemic challenges.

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:55.790
They're going to
take a long time.

00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:59.620
But what I see in this is that
this is a catalytic moment.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:01.800
These are organizations
that are not just

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:03.197
catching up with data science.

00:18:03.197 --> 00:18:05.280
They're visionary organizations
on the front lines

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:07.392
of social change
looking for all the ways

00:18:07.392 --> 00:18:08.850
that they can do
their jobs better.

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:10.800
And from something
like a weekend event

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.080
or doing some data
science projects, here

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:16.110
they're now starting this
journey into something bigger.

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:17.860
We've seen organizations
often come back

00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:20.290
from this process able to go
to their funders to say, hey,

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:21.690
we need funding
for a data science

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:23.356
because look at this
complaint registry.

00:18:23.356 --> 00:18:24.060
Or look at this.

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:25.410
Look what we can do.

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:26.246
That's huge.

00:18:26.246 --> 00:18:27.870
We see organizations
going and actually

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:30.240
now training in some of their
staff to do data science

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:33.000
or coming back and articulating
their data science and machine

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:33.875
learning problems.

00:18:33.875 --> 00:18:36.000
We've already heard that
from some of these groups.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:39.150
So I think that's what's the
second takeaway for me-- is not

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:41.440
just how much great
work gets done

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:44.490
but how it really catalyzes
these data journeys

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:45.510
for these organizations.

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:47.460
And it's so critical that
they get this resource

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:49.590
because, again, they're on the
front lines of making a better

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:50.440
world for all of us.

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:52.148
We want them to have
everything that can.

00:18:52.148 --> 00:18:53.250
ELISSE ROCHE: Absolutely.

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:56.052
Just think about it
as a proof point.

00:18:56.052 --> 00:18:57.510
With the nonprofit
model, it can be

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:59.947
difficult to secure
resources to support

00:18:59.947 --> 00:19:02.280
a variety of different projects,
especially when they're

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:03.110
trying out something new.

00:19:03.110 --> 00:19:04.901
It's great to be able
to point to something

00:19:04.901 --> 00:19:06.450
that they found successful.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:07.260
So that's great.

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:09.900
And another thing just to add
onto that and a key takeaway

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:12.700
on impact is the power
of collaboration.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:15.600
So early on in the presentation,
I discussed data for good

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:18.330
as a movement between private
companies and nonprofits.

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:19.830
And that's something
that we can see

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:21.540
take place in events
like the DataDive

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:25.050
and realizing that individuals
and companies and organizations

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:27.300
all have different
resources and abilities.

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:30.090
And so if you're actually
able to take all that together

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:32.070
and combine those
resources and abilities

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:35.730
and essentially deploy them
against a strategic project

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:39.069
that is driven by data, you
can really make a change,

00:19:39.069 --> 00:19:40.110
really make a difference.

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:41.580
So that's something
that's really inspiring.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:43.730
And being there present
at my first DataDive

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:46.230
as well while we here
hosting it Google New York,

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:47.590
I saw all that come to life.

00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:50.580
And it really was an
impactful experience for me.

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:54.120
And I believe it was that
way for the volunteers

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:56.670
as well and especially the
nonprofit organizations that

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:57.690
benefited from it.

00:19:57.690 --> 00:19:59.148
JAKE PORWAY: I
couldn't agree more.

00:19:59.148 --> 00:20:00.150
Yeah.

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.230
Just adding on that, for
anyone who's listening,

00:20:02.230 --> 00:20:03.990
there's a Burundian
quote I love, which

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:07.752
is what you do for me
without me, you do to me.

00:20:07.752 --> 00:20:09.210
And I think that's
really important

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:10.959
when thinking about
designing these things

00:20:10.959 --> 00:20:11.940
and in collaboration.

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:13.680
There's not just going
to be some tech solution

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:15.471
that I could come up
with in my room that's

00:20:15.471 --> 00:20:16.960
going to solve these problems.

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:18.060
And the nonprofits
couldn't necessarily

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:19.090
solve them on their own.

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:21.660
So if you're out there
taking your own data journey,

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:24.000
think about that
first and foremost--

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.665
not what can I build, but
who can I build it with.

00:20:26.665 --> 00:20:28.790
Because that's really the
way this stuff gets done.

00:20:28.790 --> 00:20:30.010
And I couldn't agree more.

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:31.650
I was so heartened
and enlightened

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:33.316
seeing everyone work
together and seeing

00:20:33.316 --> 00:20:35.730
that support across
corporations, nonprofits,

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:38.350
and everyone working together
to make some good stuff happen.

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:40.790
ELISSE ROCHE: Absolutely.

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:43.520
So what can you do?

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:46.070
Come together and join the
data for good movement.

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:46.820
JAKE PORWAY: Yeah.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:49.519
I'd be remiss not to mention
that, of course, at DataKind,

00:20:49.519 --> 00:20:51.810
we're always looking for
folks to come and be involved.

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:54.910
So if you're a data scientist
who wants to help out,

00:20:54.910 --> 00:20:57.300
come to datakind.org
and come get involved.

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:58.969
We have volunteer opportunities.

00:20:58.969 --> 00:20:59.760
We've got projects.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.250
There's jobs, all sorts
of stuff going on.

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:04.640
We also have meet-up
communities around the world

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:06.980
that you could join,
so please do that.

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:10.730
And we're also starting to look
for ways to go into issue areas

00:21:10.730 --> 00:21:11.430
more deeply.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.020
So keep an eye out.

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:15.680
We will be having some very
exciting projects coming up

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:16.864
very soon around that.

00:21:16.864 --> 00:21:17.780
ELISSE ROCHE: Awesome.

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:19.290
And also note from
Google Cloud--

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:21.170
so with Kaggle, if
you're a nonprofit,

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:23.810
you can apply to actually host
one of those online events

00:21:23.810 --> 00:21:26.240
to apply big data
solutions to your mission

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:27.590
or real-world challenge.

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:29.877
And if you are a data
scientist, analyst,

00:21:29.877 --> 00:21:31.460
or whomever just
looking to learn more

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:33.260
about data analytics,
you can actually

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:34.910
sign up to join the
Kaggle community

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:37.860
and get started from there.

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:39.500
So with that, thank you.

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:40.280
That's a wrap.

00:21:40.280 --> 00:22:44.590
And stay tuned for the live Q&amp;A.

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:45.370
Welcome back.

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:46.810
We have the questions.

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:48.730
So the first one--

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:52.567
how can we make data science
more accessible to the public?

00:22:52.567 --> 00:22:54.150
JAKE PORWAY: That
is a great question.

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:55.810
Let's see if we
have the answers.

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:58.630
So I think when I hear
this question, what

00:22:58.630 --> 00:23:01.660
I think about is things I've
heard people say about the fact

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:03.460
that as technologists,
we may understand

00:23:03.460 --> 00:23:06.670
what an algorithm can do or
what statistical bias is.

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:08.050
But for most people--

00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:09.650
I think about my
aunt and my grandma.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:13.570
They're walking around just
clicking apps and having

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:14.870
decisions made for them.

00:23:14.870 --> 00:23:16.495
So I think the question
a lot of people

00:23:16.495 --> 00:23:19.056
wonder about is how do
we help people understand

00:23:19.056 --> 00:23:19.930
what this stuff does.

00:23:19.930 --> 00:23:22.630
Because if we don't, we
run the risk of people

00:23:22.630 --> 00:23:25.090
not knowing if the data
is being used unethically

00:23:25.090 --> 00:23:27.910
or not being able to engage
in it, just being out there.

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:30.910
So I think a couple of
things come to mind for me.

00:23:30.910 --> 00:23:33.895
Basically, one is
webinars like this.

00:23:33.895 --> 00:23:35.770
We've got to get out
there and talk about it.

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:37.930
When you say stats
or math or data,

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:39.650
most people's eyes glaze over.

00:23:39.650 --> 00:23:41.710
But when you say,
hey, here's a way

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:45.280
that folks are using data
to empower communities

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:47.450
or to learn about when
a development project is

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:49.779
going to happen sooner,
they go, oh, OK.

00:23:49.779 --> 00:23:51.570
I never really thought
about data that way.

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:53.290
I thought about
spreadsheets or math.

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:54.490
But that's actual.

00:23:54.490 --> 00:23:55.240
That's cool.

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:56.948
So I think we have to
talk about it more.

00:23:56.948 --> 00:23:59.590
So if you're someone out there
who knows about this stuff,

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:01.390
shout it to the world.

00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:05.920
I do also think we probably need
a little more public literacy

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:09.370
around data and
around algorithms.

00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:12.070
One thing I think a lot of
people are frustrated by

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:14.980
is the fact that
so many people seem

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:18.040
to be confused by what
data does or using data

00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:22.330
to prove a political point that
maybe they shouldn't be making.

00:24:22.330 --> 00:24:25.840
And I think we haven't yet
built a public critical thinking

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:27.010
around data.

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:30.070
When you look at a newspaper
article or a picture,

00:24:30.070 --> 00:24:31.990
some people have built
this critical lens

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:34.080
about how someone
took that picture.

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:37.420
We know when we see a
provocative image, we go, OK.

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:39.550
Well, there was a photographer.

00:24:39.550 --> 00:24:41.612
They chose to take this
over something else.

00:24:41.612 --> 00:24:42.820
They probably have an agenda.

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:43.870
What's the agenda?

00:24:43.870 --> 00:24:45.700
Hopefully, people are
thinking that way.

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:48.250
But I don't think we're thinking
that way about data yet.

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:49.020
You see a chart.

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:51.936
And someone says,
45% of people are

00:24:51.936 --> 00:24:53.560
likely to die from
smoking or whatever.

00:24:53.560 --> 00:24:54.650
That doesn't make sense.

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:56.629
But I think people
go, oh, that's fact.

00:24:56.629 --> 00:24:59.170
No one thinks, well, what data
did we collect or not collect?

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:00.589
What's that person's angle?

00:25:00.589 --> 00:25:01.630
And so that's a kind of--

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:04.210
ELISSE ROCHE: Or even just
what's on the x- versus y-axis

00:25:04.210 --> 00:25:06.477
and where do the numbers
start there to actually

00:25:06.477 --> 00:25:08.560
then demonstrate a particular
point versus zooming

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:09.910
out and looking at
the whole picture.

00:25:09.910 --> 00:25:10.785
JAKE PORWAY: Exactly.

00:25:10.785 --> 00:25:13.750
There's a huge visual
literacy and total tricks

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:14.920
you can play with that.

00:25:14.920 --> 00:25:16.150
And that just takes training.

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:18.754
We just have to get
people used to that.

00:25:18.754 --> 00:25:20.170
So I think the
more, again, we can

00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:23.190
talk about it, the more
that we can educate people,

00:25:23.190 --> 00:25:25.180
maybe even-- this will
be controversial--

00:25:25.180 --> 00:25:28.270
but replace calc classes
in public education

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:29.650
with statistics classes.

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:31.970
I think that might be
more relevant these days.

00:25:31.970 --> 00:25:33.250
Things like that I think
are going to be helpful.

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:34.930
But the conversation
has to start

00:25:34.930 --> 00:25:37.179
with the folks who know it
and getting that out there.

00:25:37.179 --> 00:25:38.950
So get out there and
talk more about it.

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:40.520
I think that's going
to be really helpful.

00:25:40.520 --> 00:25:40.660
ELISSE ROCHE: Yeah.

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:42.160
I think it's also
important to think

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:45.429
about data science
versus science,

00:25:45.429 --> 00:25:47.470
because they're not too
different when you really

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:48.220
think about them.

00:25:48.220 --> 00:25:51.670
So like a scientist,
a data scientist

00:25:51.670 --> 00:25:54.310
will take observations,
will actually

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:57.340
come up with a hypothesis and
then test that hypothesis using

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:59.230
their own scientific
method against the data

00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:01.600
and against the observations
that they've collected,

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:04.850
and then seek to derive a
meaningful insight from there.

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:06.970
And I think it's
also rhetorically,

00:26:06.970 --> 00:26:09.550
looking at the term
data, thinking about what

00:26:09.550 --> 00:26:10.780
actually comprises data.

00:26:10.780 --> 00:26:12.940
It's a historical record.

00:26:12.940 --> 00:26:14.000
It's temperature.

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:17.529
It's the amount of sunlight
or rain in a particular area.

00:26:17.529 --> 00:26:18.570
It's a variety of things.

00:26:18.570 --> 00:26:20.830
It's anything that
can be recorded.

00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:22.930
If you keep your
own journal, you

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:25.030
could consider that
to be data and then

00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:27.160
use your own kind
of semantic analysis

00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:29.560
to actually see what are the
most prevalent words that

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:31.417
come up in this particular
year or something

00:26:31.417 --> 00:26:32.250
within your journal.

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:35.740
So really, data can be
anything and everything.

00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:41.585
And it doesn't have to just
be this idea of binary.

00:26:41.585 --> 00:26:42.460
JAKE PORWAY: Totally.

00:26:42.460 --> 00:26:43.310
And I'm so sorry.

00:26:43.310 --> 00:26:44.980
Before I move on to
the next question,

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:46.896
there's just one last
thing I want to it here,

00:26:46.896 --> 00:26:49.120
which is that as
data scientists,

00:26:49.120 --> 00:26:51.389
one of the things that
we can do is just share

00:26:51.389 --> 00:26:52.680
out how we're doing what we do.

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:54.250
At DataKind, one
of our values we

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:56.350
believe very strongly in
that we train everyone on

00:26:56.350 --> 00:26:57.640
is transparency.

00:26:57.640 --> 00:27:00.250
The idea is that if you are
clear about what assumptions

00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:03.070
you make, how you're using
that data, what that data is,

00:27:03.070 --> 00:27:05.141
what you're trying
to do, that's going

00:27:05.141 --> 00:27:07.390
to go a long way to people
understanding what goes on.

00:27:07.390 --> 00:27:08.223
Well, let's face it.

00:27:08.223 --> 00:27:11.320
There's some not-so-great things
going on in the image of tech

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:11.962
these days.

00:27:11.962 --> 00:27:13.420
One of the best
ways to combat that

00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:16.010
is to just be open
about what we're doing.

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:18.760
And I think that's going to help
demystify a lot of this stuff.

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:22.210
ELISSE ROCHE: I think
demystify is a really good term

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:24.130
to think about.

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:27.460
And so on the next
question, what

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:30.130
is the role of data
science, your opinion,

00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:32.170
and the humanitarian
sector, especially given

00:27:32.170 --> 00:27:34.780
your work with DataKind and
you founding the organization?

00:27:34.780 --> 00:27:38.180
I'd love personally to
hear about your intentioned

00:27:38.180 --> 00:27:40.305
thoughts around this in
founding your organization.

00:27:40.305 --> 00:27:41.679
JAKE PORWAY: Oh,
well, thank you.

00:27:41.679 --> 00:27:43.510
I would love to hear
your thoughts on this

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:45.509
as well because I know
you've seen a lot of data

00:27:45.509 --> 00:27:47.471
for humanitarian work.

00:27:47.471 --> 00:27:48.970
Look, I think it
actually comes back

00:27:48.970 --> 00:27:51.370
to what is the role of data
science, first and foremost.

00:27:51.370 --> 00:27:52.900
Like you said,
it's like science.

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:54.250
You're getting information.

00:27:54.250 --> 00:27:56.260
You're able to not
just look at the past

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:58.250
now, but sometimes
predict the future.

00:27:58.250 --> 00:28:00.460
That's a pretty
new human ability.

00:28:00.460 --> 00:28:02.110
At scale, there's
so much more data

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:04.240
that allows us to do
that and, of course,

00:28:04.240 --> 00:28:06.340
so many more things
that we can automate.

00:28:06.340 --> 00:28:09.460
Like we said in the webinar,
how many man hours--

00:28:09.460 --> 00:28:10.780
or person hours, excuse me--

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:12.999
are spent just typing
things into a computer

00:28:12.999 --> 00:28:15.040
and collecting that
information when you could be

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:16.720
out actually solving problems?

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:18.685
So that's the kind
of stuff that I

00:28:18.685 --> 00:28:21.004
think data science and
machine learning is great for.

00:28:21.004 --> 00:28:23.170
So why is that different
in the humanitarian sector?

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:25.390
I think it's actually
not that much different.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:28.090
I think anywhere someone has
a clear thing that they're

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:30.700
working on to get more people
into homeless shelters,

00:28:30.700 --> 00:28:34.570
to speed up transportation, to
prevent global warming-- all

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:38.247
that stuff is opportunities to
learn from the past from data,

00:28:38.247 --> 00:28:40.330
predict what might happen
in the future with data,

00:28:40.330 --> 00:28:42.140
and automate those processes.

00:28:42.140 --> 00:28:44.134
So I think that
opportunity is everywhere.

00:28:44.134 --> 00:28:46.300
And that really ties back
to your question about how

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:48.640
we started the organization.

00:28:48.640 --> 00:28:50.770
At the risk of sounding
a little grandiose,

00:28:50.770 --> 00:28:54.260
it almost felt immoral,
for lack of a better word,

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:56.440
that we could have this
wonderful technology that

00:28:56.440 --> 00:28:59.200
could teach us so much
more about our futures

00:28:59.200 --> 00:29:02.350
and automate what we do and
not apply it for social good.

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:04.852
If there wasn't a world
where we were seeing this,

00:29:04.852 --> 00:29:06.310
make the world a
little bit better,

00:29:06.310 --> 00:29:07.910
then what are we really doing?

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:10.330
So I think to me, what's
so exciting about this

00:29:10.330 --> 00:29:12.340
is that the whole big
data revolution has

00:29:12.340 --> 00:29:16.150
put this opportunity in the
hands of many more people

00:29:16.150 --> 00:29:17.750
across the globe
than ever before.

00:29:17.750 --> 00:29:20.540
So I think the
opportunities are endless.

00:29:20.540 --> 00:29:22.829
I think whether it's the
big lighthouse projects

00:29:22.829 --> 00:29:25.120
that you see up there that
seem really cool, like using

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:27.730
satellite imagery to track
poverty estimates, which you

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:29.780
see the World Bank
and others doing,

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:32.320
to even just the
seemingly mundane stuff,

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:34.780
like optimizing someone's
workflow so they don't have

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:36.250
to fill something out by hand--

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:38.680
all those things are resulting
in the world being better

00:29:38.680 --> 00:29:39.760
thanks to data science.

00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:40.510
That's what I see.

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:41.080
I'm curious.

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:42.602
Are you seeing similar things?

00:29:42.602 --> 00:29:43.810
Or where do you see the role?

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:44.601
ELISSE ROCHE: Yeah.

00:29:44.601 --> 00:29:46.120
I'd say that it's
important to think

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:49.474
about data analytics and
data science as a tool

00:29:49.474 --> 00:29:50.140
and as a method.

00:29:50.140 --> 00:29:52.600
So I was actually just
thinking about surveys,

00:29:52.600 --> 00:29:53.710
like qualitative surveys.

00:29:53.710 --> 00:29:55.600
And that's something
that is also a method

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:57.910
that nonprofit organizations,
companies, whomever

00:29:57.910 --> 00:29:59.170
can also employ.

00:29:59.170 --> 00:30:01.439
But what are you going to
do with a qualitative survey

00:30:01.439 --> 00:30:03.730
if you don't have essentially
the underlying foundation

00:30:03.730 --> 00:30:06.010
for what you're trying to
learn from that survey?

00:30:06.010 --> 00:30:08.320
So with that being
said, you can think

00:30:08.320 --> 00:30:12.550
about data analytics and machine
learning in the same way.

00:30:12.550 --> 00:30:14.890
And this is going back to
this idea of data science

00:30:14.890 --> 00:30:15.850
similar to science.

00:30:15.850 --> 00:30:18.830
What is the fundamental question
that you're trying to solve?

00:30:18.830 --> 00:30:22.600
What is the potential
data analytics here

00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:23.900
to help you solve that?

00:30:23.900 --> 00:30:27.164
And how can you aggregate,
organize, synthesize data

00:30:27.164 --> 00:30:28.580
in order to help
you achieve that?

00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:31.180
So something I actually heard
from the nonprofit participants

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:34.080
in the DataDive as well is
really the power of data

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:36.880
but also understanding
that it is something that

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:40.130
needs a firm and guiding hand.

00:30:40.130 --> 00:30:41.920
You need to have that
intention behind it.

00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:43.419
Because also to
your point from what

00:30:43.419 --> 00:30:47.740
were saying before
around essentially data

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:52.990
science, the reality
that you have

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:54.910
to have that
underlying intention,

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:57.970
that underlying
insight into something

00:30:57.970 --> 00:30:59.860
in order to actually
bring something to life.

00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:01.605
JAKE PORWAY: Start with
the question, not the data.

00:31:01.605 --> 00:31:03.479
I can't remember who we
ripped that off from,

00:31:03.479 --> 00:31:06.042
but it's on our
walls at the company.

00:31:06.042 --> 00:31:07.750
You got to know what
you're trying to do.

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:08.800
And then this is
in service of it.

00:31:08.800 --> 00:31:10.060
I think that's a great point.

00:31:10.060 --> 00:31:11.351
ELISSE ROCHE: Yeah, absolutely.

00:31:11.351 --> 00:31:14.860
So again, having that guidance
and also understanding,

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:17.260
developing the understanding,
and the nuances

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:18.710
of using these kinds of tools.

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:21.370
JAKE PORWAY:
Couldn't agree more.

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:22.780
ELISSE ROCHE: Next question--

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:24.670
so what skills are
needed to take part

00:31:24.670 --> 00:31:26.034
in the data for good movement?

00:31:26.034 --> 00:31:27.700
What do you look for
in your volunteers?

00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:29.150
JAKE PORWAY: Oh, such
a great question.

00:31:29.150 --> 00:31:30.858
I think when people
hear this they think,

00:31:30.858 --> 00:31:33.830
oh, so I've got to be some
AI expert, machine learning

00:31:33.830 --> 00:31:34.330
engineer.

00:31:34.330 --> 00:31:36.130
And yes, there's
a place for that.

00:31:36.130 --> 00:31:37.241
We definitely need that.

00:31:37.241 --> 00:31:38.740
But when I actually
think about what

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:41.190
the hardest parts
of this work are,

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:44.930
it's really around
communication and collaboration.

00:31:44.930 --> 00:31:46.240
So imagine this.

00:31:46.240 --> 00:31:49.720
If you're a data scientist, you
probably do work for a company.

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:53.452
And they have data that comes in
through infrastructure and QA.

00:31:53.452 --> 00:31:54.910
And you've got
business objectives.

00:31:54.910 --> 00:31:58.210
And you can just get in there
and tweak things, optimize,

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:00.280
build some models on
some nice, clean data.

00:32:00.280 --> 00:32:02.860
Now imagine that you're thrust
into a nonprofit organization

00:32:02.860 --> 00:32:05.110
that does not have that data
infrastructure where they

00:32:05.110 --> 00:32:07.645
cannot necessarily articulate
their data science problem.

00:32:07.645 --> 00:32:10.510
All of a sudden, you've got
ethical factors weighing in.

00:32:10.510 --> 00:32:12.940
You've got people who have
tons of different constraints

00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:13.780
on them.

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:16.030
The skills you start
to need are the tools

00:32:16.030 --> 00:32:17.980
like being able to
hear someone's request

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:21.120
and really understand what
the underlying need is.

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:23.620
The Red Cross project that we
spoke about before is actually

00:32:23.620 --> 00:32:24.310
a good example.

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:27.640
So when we first came to
them, the question was,

00:32:27.640 --> 00:32:29.980
how do we upgrade our databases?

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:31.640
That's our data problem.

00:32:31.640 --> 00:32:34.870
And sure, those databases
probably needed to be upgraded.

00:32:34.870 --> 00:32:35.920
Could have done that.

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:37.960
But it took some
data scientists with

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:41.002
a smart, analytical mindset to
step back and, to your point,

00:32:41.002 --> 00:32:41.710
ask the question.

00:32:41.710 --> 00:32:43.180
Start with the
question, not the data.

00:32:43.180 --> 00:32:44.700
And they said, well, what
are you trying to do?

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:46.658
And that's how they
eventually got to this idea

00:32:46.658 --> 00:32:48.180
that we're trying
to prevent fires.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:50.080
We need to put the
smoke alarms somewhere.

00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:51.121
Well, how do you do that?

00:32:51.121 --> 00:32:51.872
We're not sure.

00:32:51.872 --> 00:32:53.830
That's how they found
the data science problem.

00:32:53.830 --> 00:32:55.560
So I would say that's
actually really

00:32:55.560 --> 00:32:58.037
some of the most critical
skills, whether with DataKind

00:32:58.037 --> 00:33:00.120
or anyone else in the data
for good movement, that

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:01.050
are needed--

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:03.780
being able to hear a
problem and understand

00:33:03.780 --> 00:33:07.161
what the solution could be, to
combat a problem with humility,

00:33:07.161 --> 00:33:09.410
to realize that we may know
technology, but that's it.

00:33:09.410 --> 00:33:12.600
These other people know the
space, their issue area,

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:13.880
the people involved.

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:15.880
To come in and not think,
I've got the solution,

00:33:15.880 --> 00:33:18.900
but instead saying, hey, maybe
I could be some support--

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:20.990
I think that humility
is a big role.

00:33:20.990 --> 00:33:23.100
And then being able
to communicate across

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:25.350
all the technologists
and non-technologists.

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:26.710
I think those are huge.

00:33:26.710 --> 00:33:28.890
So I would say as
much as the work we do

00:33:28.890 --> 00:33:31.580
is around tech-- and need
expert technologists--

00:33:31.580 --> 00:33:34.230
those other kind of
critical thinking

00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:38.340
and collaborative communication
skills are almost worth more.

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:39.630
So I'd say bring those.

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:41.066
And also, if you're
feeling like,

00:33:41.066 --> 00:33:42.690
yeah, but what if
I'm not in that boat,

00:33:42.690 --> 00:33:43.740
either, hey, don't worry.

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:45.960
This whole thing is a
big pro bono operation.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:48.690
We need people who are project
managers, communications

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:51.340
experts, UX designers.

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:54.180
There's a role for almost
anyone in this movement

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:56.305
because, again, it
touches all of us.

00:33:56.305 --> 00:33:58.290
We're shaping society together.

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:00.409
So really, there's
room for everyone.

00:34:00.409 --> 00:34:01.700
ELISSE ROCHE: Yeah, absolutely.

00:34:01.700 --> 00:34:03.900
So you said something I
was thinking about myself--

00:34:03.900 --> 00:34:07.437
critical thinking, being
a critical thinker.

00:34:07.437 --> 00:34:09.270
Even though we are
talking about technology,

00:34:09.270 --> 00:34:11.280
we are talking about data
analytics and science,

00:34:11.280 --> 00:34:12.900
you don't necessarily
need to have

00:34:12.900 --> 00:34:16.830
a technical background of
really any kind to participate.

00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:19.969
Because you were even
mentioning before consulting--

00:34:19.969 --> 00:34:22.590
this idea of having consulting
experience or something

00:34:22.590 --> 00:34:24.050
could absolutely be useful.

00:34:24.050 --> 00:34:24.925
JAKE PORWAY: Totally.

00:34:24.925 --> 00:34:26.709
All those management
consultant frameworks

00:34:26.709 --> 00:34:27.750
that everyone laughs at--

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:29.416
they're really useful
for getting people

00:34:29.416 --> 00:34:30.739
to think through a problem.

00:34:30.739 --> 00:34:33.030
That's where you have to
start to get this stuff right.

00:34:33.030 --> 00:34:34.510
ELISSE ROCHE: And also, I'd
say research experience.

00:34:34.510 --> 00:34:36.420
So if you have conducted
any qualitative

00:34:36.420 --> 00:34:39.060
or quantitative research
outside of data science,

00:34:39.060 --> 00:34:40.650
that's something
you can also employ.

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:43.090
The ability to synthesize
large amounts of information,

00:34:43.090 --> 00:34:45.000
especially if they're
provided by said

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:47.622
nonprofit organizations,
so you can then parse out

00:34:47.622 --> 00:34:49.830
those individual questions
that we were talking about

00:34:49.830 --> 00:34:51.621
before that are critical
for data science--

00:34:51.621 --> 00:34:53.699
I'd say that's
also something that

00:34:53.699 --> 00:34:56.880
is a very useful skill in
the data for good movement.

00:34:56.880 --> 00:34:59.490
And also, design--
so increasingly,

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:04.200
data visualizations are very
important to expressing data.

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:07.920
Because if you think about
it, as an average person

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:10.380
or consumer, if a
scientific report comes out,

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:12.070
that's not necessarily
reaching you.

00:35:12.070 --> 00:35:13.611
And if it is reaching
you, it's often

00:35:13.611 --> 00:35:15.480
distilled in a series of ways.

00:35:15.480 --> 00:35:17.820
And similarly, data
analysis can then

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:21.940
be achieved or published in
a certain way within a niche

00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:22.440
group.

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:25.479
But it's not necessarily
reaching the average person.

00:35:25.479 --> 00:35:27.270
So with that being
said, data visualization

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:28.800
actually comes into play there.

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:31.205
That's incredibly useful
to synthesize information.

00:35:31.205 --> 00:35:32.580
That's something
you can actually

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:35.580
do with Data Studio on the
Google Cloud side, which

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:38.430
connects to Google BigQuery
where you could, for example,

00:35:38.430 --> 00:35:41.400
analyze the GCP public data
sets that are available,

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:44.430
as I mentioned before, from the
World Bank, EPA, NOA, et cetera

00:35:44.430 --> 00:35:45.930
and then visualize
that information.

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:48.210
Explore and see what you
can find in Data Studio

00:35:48.210 --> 00:35:49.300
because that helps
to drive action.

00:35:49.300 --> 00:35:50.250
You have a dashboard.

00:35:50.250 --> 00:35:54.742
You have these clean charts
and maps and whatever you'd

00:35:54.742 --> 00:35:55.950
like to customize from there.

00:35:55.950 --> 00:35:57.210
And you can share
it with others.

00:35:57.210 --> 00:35:58.190
JAKE PORWAY: Very cool.

00:35:58.190 --> 00:36:00.060
We have maybe time for one more.

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:01.605
Or do you want to
do both lightning?

00:36:01.605 --> 00:36:02.630
ELISSE ROCHE: Let's see.

00:36:02.630 --> 00:36:03.570
Do you want to
try both lighting?

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:03.920
JAKE PORWAY: All right.

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:04.020
Let's go for it.

00:36:04.020 --> 00:36:04.270
Lightning round.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:06.200
ELISSE ROCHE: We've
been waxing a lot.

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:08.310
So next question--
where can data science

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:09.427
make the most impact?

00:36:09.427 --> 00:36:11.010
JAKE PORWAY: This
is a great question.

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:12.960
People always ask, what's
the issue area to go into?

00:36:12.960 --> 00:36:13.740
ELISSE ROCHE:
That's a tough one.

00:36:13.740 --> 00:36:15.300
JAKE PORWAY: I would say
there's almost no limit.

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:16.841
Because as we
mentioned, data science

00:36:16.841 --> 00:36:19.140
can help with seeing the
future and automating it.

00:36:19.140 --> 00:36:20.700
And you can use that everywhere.

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:23.520
I will say I think we see
great opportunities wherever

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:26.280
there is a really quantifiable
outcome that everyone's

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:26.850
agreed on.

00:36:26.850 --> 00:36:30.420
So your health outcomes,
like eliminating a disease,

00:36:30.420 --> 00:36:32.232
food transport, water--

00:36:32.232 --> 00:36:33.690
those are a little
easier than some

00:36:33.690 --> 00:36:37.230
of the more human rights
advocacy-level kind

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:38.010
of projects.

00:36:38.010 --> 00:36:40.425
But there's still
opportunity there, too.

00:36:40.425 --> 00:36:41.800
But I think maybe
one of the ways

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:43.966
I'm going to spin this,
even in the lightning round,

00:36:43.966 --> 00:36:47.441
is what considerations you have
to think of in different issue

00:36:47.441 --> 00:36:47.940
areas.

00:36:47.940 --> 00:36:51.597
Because, remember, places that
are working for human rights--

00:36:51.597 --> 00:36:53.430
data has been used as
a weapon against them.

00:36:53.430 --> 00:36:55.450
When you get data about
vulnerable communities,

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:57.370
governments use
that against folks.

00:36:57.370 --> 00:36:59.850
So there's still great room
for data science to be used.

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:02.100
But you've got to go about
in a totally different way.

00:37:02.100 --> 00:37:04.350
There has to be a huge
lens on privacy, ethics,

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:05.130
and consideration.

00:37:05.130 --> 00:37:07.042
You really have to work
through those groups

00:37:07.042 --> 00:37:09.000
to understand how to
protect these communities.

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:10.374
So maybe that's
what I would say.

00:37:10.374 --> 00:37:11.680
There's room everywhere for it.

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:13.110
There's so many opportunities.

00:37:13.110 --> 00:37:15.609
But you might have to take a
slightly different tack in each

00:37:15.609 --> 00:37:16.770
of the different areas.

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:16.920
ELISSE ROCHE: Yeah.

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:19.710
I would say quickly I think
that a huge opportunity for data

00:37:19.710 --> 00:37:22.334
science and analytics
is the environment.

00:37:22.334 --> 00:37:23.750
That's something
that you can see.

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:26.000
You mentioned before World
Bank and satellite imagery.

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:28.790
Well, satellite imagery can
also be used to, for example,

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:30.990
track deforestation
in the Amazon

00:37:30.990 --> 00:37:33.210
or in other highly
biodiverse regions.

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:35.415
That's something that
Google Earth Outreach

00:37:35.415 --> 00:37:37.290
has been working on with
different indigenous

00:37:37.290 --> 00:37:40.020
organizations and protecting
their ancestral lands.

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:42.720
And that's something that
data analytics, analysis,

00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:47.710
GIS-- that all can
be supremely helpful.

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:50.620
And last question--
so how did DataKind

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:52.990
select their engagement models?

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:54.484
And what's next?

00:37:54.484 --> 00:37:55.150
JAKE PORWAY: OK.

00:37:55.150 --> 00:37:56.350
Let's think of it this way.

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:58.180
The whole purpose
of this organization

00:37:58.180 --> 00:38:01.090
is to see data science applied
to any social challenge

00:38:01.090 --> 00:38:02.500
where it could be useful.

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:04.120
And so we picked
engagement models

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:06.700
that could tap into existing
talent and just kind of sew it

00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:08.450
together as easy as possible.

00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:08.950
We laugh.

00:38:08.950 --> 00:38:10.810
We often say we're
the weaver of worlds.

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:13.090
We don't have a lot of
technologists on staff

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:15.352
because most of our job is
just coordinating experts

00:38:15.352 --> 00:38:16.810
in the social sector
and technology

00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:18.400
to come together
to do great things.

00:38:18.400 --> 00:38:19.900
So we've picked
them that way to try

00:38:19.900 --> 00:38:22.630
to make sure that
people have the least

00:38:22.630 --> 00:38:26.829
overhead to helping from
either side as they want to.

00:38:26.829 --> 00:38:28.870
So when you ask what's
next, all I could think of

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:30.719
is we're working
to scale that out.

00:38:30.719 --> 00:38:31.510
So we want to know.

00:38:31.510 --> 00:38:34.093
If you're out there and you want
to build a DataKind community

00:38:34.093 --> 00:38:37.410
on your own, let us know.

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:39.190
There's some exciting
news coming out.

00:38:39.190 --> 00:38:40.870
We're working with a
foundation to be thinking

00:38:40.870 --> 00:38:43.180
in the next few months about how
we can grow out and replicate

00:38:43.180 --> 00:38:43.690
this model.

00:38:43.690 --> 00:38:46.720
So I would basically just leave
this as an open call people

00:38:46.720 --> 00:38:50.521
to say if you're a corporation,
a university, nonprofit,

00:38:50.521 --> 00:38:53.020
anyone else who wants to see
more data for good in the world

00:38:53.020 --> 00:38:55.609
and wants to think about how
to scale it, come talk to us.

00:38:55.609 --> 00:38:57.400
Come to datakind.org
and write us an email.

00:38:57.400 --> 00:38:59.270
Because we've got
some big plans there

00:38:59.270 --> 00:39:01.090
and we'd love you all
to be a part of it.

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:03.310
ELISSE ROCHE: And you also
have several chapters, right?

00:39:03.310 --> 00:39:03.770
JAKE PORWAY: We do.

00:39:03.770 --> 00:39:05.610
We have volunteer
chapters around the world.

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:07.360
We've been learning
from the five we have.

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:09.670
They're in places that are rich
in both nonprofits and tech.

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:11.110
But we want to take
it beyond that.

00:39:11.110 --> 00:39:13.485
How do we get this in every
city, every organization that

00:39:13.485 --> 00:39:14.470
wants it?

00:39:14.470 --> 00:39:17.385
So, again, if you have ideas
on that, come talk with us.

00:39:17.385 --> 00:39:18.510
ELISSE ROCHE: That's great.

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:20.200
Well, thank you so
much for your time

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:22.450
and for joining me here
in the New York office.

00:39:22.450 --> 00:39:22.980
JAKE PORWAY: Thank you.

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:23.240
It's been great.

00:39:23.240 --> 00:39:23.680
ELISSE ROCHE: It
was really great

00:39:23.680 --> 00:39:25.846
just talking about the
DataDive and the work that we

00:39:25.846 --> 00:39:27.360
were able to do together.

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:30.284
And so we're actually at
time for this webinar.

00:39:30.284 --> 00:39:31.700
So with that being
said, we'd like

00:39:31.700 --> 00:39:33.520
to thank you all
out there as well

00:39:33.520 --> 00:39:35.650
and say stay tuned
for the next session.

00:39:35.650 --> 00:39:38.740
Because what's coming up
next is accelerating insights

00:39:38.740 --> 00:39:41.230
with external data sets on GCP.

00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:41.980
Thanks again.

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:43.180
JAKE PORWAY: Thanks.

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:46.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

